---
/**
 * The terminology lexicon index.
 *
 * WHAT THIS IS FOR. Ayurvedic technical terms are rendered into English inconsistently, and the
 * inconsistency is not a translation quibble: `doṣa` as "humour" imports a Galenic theory that is
 * not what the word means, and `praśamana` as "pacifying" conceals that the endpoint is
 * equilibrium rather than reduction. So each term here carries every rendering found for it, with
 * the source that uses it and an assessment of whether the rendering survives the classical
 * passage it claims to render.
 *
 * WHAT IS NOT HERE. The concept records, which cover the theory of health and disease as wholes
 * rather than term by term. When this page was written they held 408 quoted classical citations
 * with not one checked against its source, and publishing them beside the terms would have
 * borrowed the word verified. They have since paid for it: 207 citations read against their
 * sources, 29 refuted, 33 repairs applied, and they now publish at /concept/, with the four
 * records whose repairs are unsettled still held back. Component terms cross-link both ways.
 */
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import lexicon from '../../data/lexicon.json';
import conceptsData from '../../data/concepts.json';
import { abs, rel, datasetIdentity } from '../../lib/site';

const data = lexicon as any;
const records = data.records as any[];
const families = data.families as { id: string; label: string }[];
const sum = data.summary;

const title = 'Ayurvedic terminology: how each term is rendered into English';
const description = `${records.length} Ayurvedic technical terms with every English rendering found `
  + 'for each, the source that uses it, an assessment of whether the rendering holds, and the '
  + 'classical passages that bear on the term. Every quoted classical citation checked against its source.';
const canonical = '/lexicon/';

const n = (x: number) => x.toLocaleString('en-GB');
const byFamily = families.map((f) => ({
  ...f,
  items: records.filter((r) => r.family === f.id).sort((a, b) => a.slug.localeCompare(b.slug)),
}));
const unplaced = records.filter((r) => !r.family);
const con = (conceptsData as any).summary;

/** Terms whose renderings disagree with each other, which are the interesting ones. */
const contested = records
  .map((r) => ({ r, distinct: new Set(r.renderings.map((x: any) => String(x.english ?? '').toLowerCase().trim())).size }))
  .filter((x) => x.distinct >= 4)
  .sort((a, b) => b.distinct - a.distinct)
  .slice(0, 24);

const withMistranslations = records.filter((r) => (r.mistranslations ?? []).length);

const jsonld = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'DefinedTermSet',
    '@id': `${abs(canonical)}#termset`,
    name: title,
    description,
    url: abs(canonical),
    license: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
    inLanguage: ['en', 'sa'],
    creator: { '@id': `${abs('/')}#organization` },
    publisher: { '@id': `${abs('/')}#organization` },
    hasDefinedTerm: records.map((r) => ({
      '@type': 'DefinedTerm',
      '@id': `${abs(`/lexicon/${r.slug}/`)}#term`,
      name: r.iast ?? r.slug,
      alternateName: [r.devanagari, ...(r.alternates ?? [])].filter(Boolean),
      description: r.renderings?.[0]?.english ?? undefined,
      url: abs(`/lexicon/${r.slug}/`),
      inDefinedTermSet: { '@id': `${abs(canonical)}#termset` },
    })),
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Dataset',
    ...datasetIdentity(),
    '@id': `${abs(canonical)}#dataset`,
    name: data.name,
    description: data.description,
    url: abs(canonical),
    license: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
    isAccessibleForFree: true,
    creator: { '@id': `${abs('/')}#organization` },
    publisher: { '@id': `${abs('/')}#organization` },
    variableMeasured: ['term', 'English rendering', 'rendering source', 'assessment', 'classical citation'],
    distribution: [
      { '@type': 'DataDownload', name: 'JSON', encodingFormat: 'application/json', contentUrl: abs('/lexicon.json') },
      { '@type': 'DataDownload', name: 'CSV', encodingFormat: 'text/csv', contentUrl: abs('/lexicon.csv') },
    ],
  },
];
---
<Page title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} updated={data.updatedAt}
  trail={[{ label: 'Lexicon' }]} jsonld={jsonld}>
  <p class="answer">
    Ayurvedic technical terms are rendered into English inconsistently, and the inconsistency
    changes the meaning. This lexicon holds <strong>{n(records.length)} terms</strong> with
    <strong>{n(sum.renderings)} renderings</strong> between them: for each term, every English
    equivalent found in the WHO standard terminologies, the published Samhita translations and the
    secondary literature, each with the source that uses it and an assessment of whether it
    survives the classical passage it claims to render.
  </p>

  <p class="lede">
    The point is not to pick a winner. It is that a reader meeting "humour" for
    <i>doṣa</i> or "pacifying" for <i>praśamana</i> should be able to see what the word carries
    that the English does not.
  </p>

  <table class="facts">
    <caption>What is here</caption>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Terms</th><td>{n(records.length)}</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">English renderings recorded</th><td>{n(sum.renderings)}</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Classical citations</th><td>{n(sum.citations)}</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Quotations published</th><td>{n(sum.quotesPublished)}</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Quotations withheld</th><td>{n(sum.quotesWithheld)}, each saying why on the citation</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Citations that are an address only</th><td>{n(sum.citationsThatAreAddressOnly)}</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Terms with a recorded mistranslation</th><td>{n(withMistranslations.length)}</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Which quotations are published, and which are not</h2>
  <p>
    A classical quotation appears here only if it is Sanskrit <i>mūla</i>, which is ancient and
    carries no licence question, or an English translation this project has cleared:
    Kaviratna's <cite>Charaka</cite> of 1896 to 1913, or Bhishagratna's <cite>Sushruta</cite> of
    1907 to 1916. Anything else is published as an address and our own account of what the passage
    establishes, with the quoted words withheld and the reason stated on the citation itself.
    {sum.quotesWithheld} of {n(sum.citations)} citations are in that position.
  </p>
  <p>
    A quotation is also withheld if nobody has read it against its source, whatever its licence
    says. That accounts for most of the withheld ones: the verification pass keyed on a field that
    these citations do not carry, so they escaped it, and an unchecked quotation is not something
    this site will stand behind. The address and the characterisation still publish, because a
    reader can follow an address to a library.
  </p>

  <h2>The companion set: where the theory disagrees with itself</h2>
  <p>
    A term entry answers what a word means. It does not answer whether the thing the word names is
    settled, and often it is not. The
    <a href={rel('/concept/')}>concept records</a> hold {n(con.contestedQuestions)} questions on
    which the classical sources genuinely disagree, with {n(con.attributedPositions)} competing
    positions attributed to the sources that hold them, plus {n(con.notKnownStatements)} explicit
    statements of what is not established. {n(con.componentsLinkedToLexicon)} of the terms here are
    named as components of one of those concepts and link both ways.
  </p>

  <h2>Terms whose renderings disagree most</h2>
  <p>
    Four or more distinct English equivalents in use for one term. These are where the translation
    question has teeth.
  </p>
  <ul class="grid">
    {contested.map(({ r, distinct }) => (
      <li class="card">
        <a href={rel(`/lexicon/${r.slug}/`)}>{r.iast ?? r.slug}</a>
        {r.devanagari && <> <span class="deva">{r.devanagari}</span></>}
        <p><strong>{distinct}</strong> distinct renderings. {r.renderings[0]?.english && <>Including "{r.renderings[0].english}".</>}</p>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h2>Browse by subject</h2>
  <p>
    These groupings are editorial, not classical. The formulary and the Samhitas do not sort their
    vocabulary this way; each term's own category, as recorded during gathering, is printed on its
    page.
  </p>
  {byFamily.map((f) => (
    <>
      <h3>{f.label} <span class="count">{f.items.length}</span></h3>
      <p class="termlist">
        {f.items.map((r, i) => (
          <>{i > 0 && ' · '}<a href={rel(`/lexicon/${r.slug}/`)}>{r.iast ?? r.slug}</a></>
        ))}
      </p>
    </>
  ))}
  {unplaced.length > 0 && (
    <>
      <h3>Not yet placed in a group <span class="count">{unplaced.length}</span></h3>
      <p class="termlist">
        {unplaced.map((r, i) => (
          <>{i > 0 && ' · '}<a href={rel(`/lexicon/${r.slug}/`)}>{r.iast ?? r.slug}</a></>
        ))}
      </p>
    </>
  )}

  <h2>Download</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={rel('/lexicon.json')}>lexicon.json</a> and
    <a href={rel('/lexicon.csv')}>lexicon.csv</a>, CC BY 4.0. The CSV is one row per rendering,
    which is the shape anyone comparing translations wants. Our assessments are ours and are
    included; withheld quotations are absent from both and the reason travels with the row.
  </p>

  <p class="meta">
    Exported {data.updatedAt} from the term corpus, so this cannot drift from it.
    Machine-readable twin of every term page: append <code>.md</code> to its URL.
  </p>
</Page>

<style>
  .deva { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: .95em; }
  .count { color: var(--ink-soft); font-weight: 400; font-size: .8em; }
  .termlist { line-height: 2; font-size: .95rem; }
</style>
